3. Design philosophy to apply

3.1 Visual hierarchy

Each screen must have a clear primary focus.

Apply this principle according to the screen's actual purpose:

Screen| Primary visual focus
Dashboard| Today's training protocol or restored active session
Session| Current exercise and set-entry controls
History| Chronological training records and selected report
Analytics| Estimated 1RM progression
Manage| Program identity and configuration
Embedded Calendar| Selected date and its training status

Do not make every section, card, heading, badge and metric equally prominent.

Use visual prominence deliberately:

- Primary information: highest contrast and clearest hierarchy.
- Secondary information: readable but visually quieter.
- Metadata: compact and subdued.
- Decorative elements: remove unless they communicate useful information.

One prominent orange action or focal treatment is generally enough per screen. Other elements should not compete with it.

3.2 Surfaces and containers

Use the existing surface system.

- "SURFACE.canvas": application background.
- "SURFACE.default": standard cards.
- "SURFACE.subtle": subtle grouping.
- "SURFACE.recessed": nested data or supporting content.
- "SURFACE.elevated": genuine floating or elevated elements.
- Existing dialog and overlay treatments: retain where functionally necessary.

Rules:

- Do not place cards inside cards without a meaningful grouping or interaction boundary.
- Do not give every section a strong border, shadow or accent.
- Standard cards should remain flat, using background and border for separation.
- Reserve elevated surfaces for genuine overlays, dialogs and floating panels.
- Remove decorative glow from ordinary content.
- Do not introduce glass cards or gradient card backgrounds.
- Retain the existing sticky navigation and overlay treatment where it serves legibility.

Use "SPACING.section" for genuinely prominent modules, "SPACING.standard" for ordinary sections and "SPACING.compact" for dense supporting content where appropriate.

Do not blindly reduce all spacing. Preserve readability and touch accessibility.

3.3 Radius discipline

Use the semantic radius tokens:

- "RADIUS.badge" for badges and compact labels.
- "RADIUS.button" for controls and inputs.
- "RADIUS.card" for cards.
- "RADIUS.panel" for dialogs and floating panels.
- "RADIUS.pill" only for genuinely pill-shaped elements, circular avatars or indicators, and the established active mobile navigation treatment.

Specific shared-component correction:

"src/components/ui/Badge.tsx" currently uses "RADIUS.pill", even though the canonical "RADIUS.badge" is defined for badges.

Change the Badge primitive to use "RADIUS.badge". Check all consumers to ensure that badge text, dots, icons and status colors remain correctly aligned.

Keep the active mobile navigation pill. It is an existing, tested navigation affordance, not permission to make all UI elements pill-shaped.

Clarify the intended exceptions in "AGENTS.md" / "DESIGN_SYSTEM.md" so that the zero-pill discipline cannot be interpreted as removing the established active navigation treatment.

3.4 Typography

Use only the existing "TYPOGRAPHY" tokens unless an actual semantic gap is identified.

Apply the existing fonts intentionally:

- Bebas Neue: page titles, major section headings and prominent metric values.
- DM Sans: readable descriptions, supporting explanations, instructions and body content.
- JetBrains Mono: weights, reps, durations, percentages, dates, metrics and technical metadata.

Rules:

- Reserve display typography for meaningful hierarchy.
- Do not use large display headings for ordinary list entries.
- Keep comparable numbers tabular and consistently aligned.
- Avoid excessive uppercase and tracking in sentences users need to read.
- Do not shrink important labels or descriptions to achieve density.
- Preserve the existing typography floors; never introduce text smaller than 9px.
- Ensure long exercise names and values wrap intentionally on narrow screens.

Use page-level typography for actual page titles, section-level typography for major sections and subsection typography for cards and list entries.

3.5 Color discipline

Neutral is the default.

Use semantic colors only when their meaning applies.

- Orange: brand, primary action, achievement and PR.
- Emerald: successful states and approved data visualization.
- Amber: warning, caution and partial completion.
- Red: failure, destructive actions and missed states.
- Indigo: intelligence and strength-trend analysis.
- Zinc: neutral and unclassified information.

Do not color every metric card simply because an accent option exists.

Do not use a color alone to communicate a state. Preserve labels, numbers or other understandable indicators.

Workout-type colors must remain distinct from semantic status colors.

3.6 Motion

Use the existing "DURATION", "TRANSITION" and "MOTION_CONFIG" definitions.

Do not introduce a second motion system.

Specific rules:

- No repeated decorative entrance animation for every section.
- No large-scale slide, scale or stagger animation during ordinary tab navigation.
- No unnecessary animation of dimensions or layout.
- No decorative pulse, shimmer or looping animation.
- Do not delay the display of metrics or the availability of controls for animation.
- Keep reduced-motion behavior.
- Preserve functional transitions such as exercise expansion, selected states, dialogs and meaningful completion feedback.
- Preserve the existing 500ms set-completion acknowledgement flash in SessionView.
- Do not change navigation, lazy loading, prewarming or scroll restoration as part of this visual task.

For ordinary static metrics, avoid replaying count-up animations every time a tab is mounted. Use the existing "disableAnimation" capability where appropriate. Retain the StatCard's existing animation behavior for cases where a value genuinely changes and the animation improves feedback.

